    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey can u explain how a capacitor works in an ac circuit compared to dc? specifically why does it block dc but let ac through? also what is the formula for capacitive reactance?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In an AC circuit, a capacitor stores energy solely in its electric field and allows AC to pass through, enabling the charging and discharge of the capacitor. This is because the waveform of AC has both positive and negative phases, allowing the capacitor to maintain its charge.\n\nIn a DC circuit, the capacitor cannot store energy in its magnetic field (since there is no changing current), and as a result, DC simply passes through without being affected.\n\nThe formula for capacitive reactance (Xc) is:\n\nXc = 1 / (2πfC)\n\nwhere:\n- Xc is the capacitive reactance in ohms\n- f is the frequency of the AC in hertz (Hz)\n- C is the capacitance in farads",
